"Arranges a concise problem-solving argument from current conditions and causes to countermeasures, implementation and follow-up.\n\nUse when: Build shared understanding of a bounded improvement problem. Review the reasoning behind an intervention before expanding it.\n\nAvoid when: The problem requires a large evidence archive or several unrelated investigations on one sheet.\n\nVisual grammar: Labeled panels separate problem context, current condition, target, analysis, action and follow-up. Small diagrams support the argument within relevant panels. A clear reading order connects evidence to proposed countermeasures.\n\nBuild: State the performance gap and show observed current conditions. Investigate causes before selecting targeted countermeasures. Record implementation responsibilities and how results will be checked.\n\nCheck: Countermeasures address supported causes rather than symptoms alone. Targets include observable completion or improvement criteria. Follow-up explains how learning will change the next action.\n\nPitfalls: Shrinking a long report until it physically fits the page. Writing the proposed solution before understanding the problem.\n\nExample: A workshop investigates late tool returns, maps the handoff problem and tests a checkout change with a clear follow-up review.\n\nAlternatives: Fishbone diagram, Value-stream map."
